&lt;/figure&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Writing diagrams&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="writing-diagrams"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#writing-diagrams" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use &lt;code&gt;.&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;'&lt;/code&gt; for rounded corners, &lt;code&gt;-&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;|&lt;/code&gt; for lines, &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; for junctions
and &lt;code&gt;o&lt;/code&gt; for terminals. See the
&lt;a
href="https://ivanceras.github.io/svgbob-editor/"target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Svgbob reference&lt;/a&gt; for all shapes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use spaces, not tabs, and keep lines below 100 characters.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Add a &lt;code&gt;title&lt;/code&gt; for screen readers: &lt;code&gt;```svgbob {title=&amp;quot;Relay wiring&amp;quot;}&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Wiring diagram rules&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="wiring-diagram-rules"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#wiring-diagram-rules" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Draw a box per device with one &lt;code&gt;o&lt;/code&gt; terminal per pin, and label every pin the
way the device is labelled (&lt;code&gt;GPIO25&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;VDD&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;SDA&lt;/code&gt;).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Draw components such as resistors as a small box on the wire, entered at the
centre column: &lt;code&gt;.--+--.&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;|4.7k |&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;'--+--'&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Give every device its own DATA, VDD and GND wires. A &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; junction may only join
wires of the same net. The pull-up of a DS18B20 is the only link between DATA
and 3V3.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Keep the 230V side out of low-voltage drawings and link to the
&lt;a
href="https://smart-swimmingpool.com/docs/components/pool-controller/electrical-safety/"&gt;electrical safety guide&lt;/a&gt; instead.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Avoid &lt;code&gt;(&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;)&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;/&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;\&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; and a single &lt;code&gt;-&lt;/code&gt; inside labels, because
Svgbob turns them into arcs, diagonals or arrows. Write &lt;code&gt;2 channels&lt;/code&gt;, not
&lt;code&gt;2-channel&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Draw one diagram per subsystem instead of one large drawing.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Pad all rows of a box to the same length so the right border lines up.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;Hosting&lt;span class="hx:absolute hx:-mt-20" id="hosting"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;a href="#hosting" class="subheading-anchor" aria-label="Permalink for this section"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Hugo build sends each diagram once to the Kroki instance configured in
&lt;code&gt;hugo.yaml&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;params.kroki.url&lt;/code&gt;, default &lt;code&gt;https://kroki.io&lt;/code&gt;) and caches the
result. Visitors never contact Kroki. To keep diagram sources private, run your
own instance (&lt;code&gt;docker run -p 8000:8000 yuzutech/kroki&lt;/code&gt;) and point &lt;code&gt;kroki.url&lt;/code&gt;
to it. If Kroki is unreachable, the block falls back to plain text and the build
prints a warning.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>